From caf-talk Caf Jan 28 10:14:21 1993
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan)
Subject: Article 2--Re: Cornell changes computer speech policy
Message-ID: <C1KKv9.99M@ms.uky.edu>
Date: Thu, 28 Jan 1993 15:11:32 GMT

escheire@sunlab.cit.cornell.edu (Eric Scheirer) wrote:
>Excerpted from this morning's Cornell Daily Sun:

I realize that Cornell is a private university, but I would hope that
they endorse the very freedoms that allow them to exist as such.....

>According to the two-page document, the University "reserves the right to dis-
>connect from its networks those computers (whether or not owned by the
>University) used to transmit material in violation of university policies
>or codes, or state or federal laws."  

One user's transmission of, say, a sexually explicit GIF, could cause the
disconnection of system with 1000 users?  Yikes!  Do they realize that
"disconnect from its networks" includes things like email, NFS file sharing,
and other essential services that could impede both other systems on campus
and faculty/staff/student work?

What about the other 999 users of our hypothetical system?  Ouch!

>Lynn said that access to computer
>networks would be temporarily closed, only in the case of files containing
>obscene or offensive material, such as sexually explicit screen savers and
>an anti-Semitic role-playing game.  If the injunction is reversed by campus
>judiciary procedures, he added, the files would be reopened immediately.

If you have to do something (and that's debatable), why not temporarily 
encrypt/move the file(s), rather than cutting the network lines?  Someone 
*please* tell me that I'm misreading this......

>Although the code aims to protect the civil rights of members of the Cornell
>community, serveral Assembly members expressed reservations about the policy.
>The proposal calls for value judgements on the part of information technologies
>officials.

Darned right!  I'm a systems administrator, not a judge!  I have neither the
desire nor the time to filter through Usenet submissions, email messages,
and user files.  Bleah!  As many of you know, I have some rather strong 
opinions about many things; however, you can't expect me to apply my personal
code of ethics/morals to the 2100 users of my systems.

>"Is the person making the decision qualified?  We need to determine that on a 
>case-by-case basis."

I'll tell you (up front) that I, for one, would not be qualified.  This 
university (University of Kentucky) has students, faculty and staff from
around the world; how can I possible know and understand the culture of
each of them?  How can I anticipate what they (or even a majority of them)
would find obscene or objectionable?  

If memory serves, wouldn't a devout Muslim be severely offended by women 
wearing short skirts and low-cut dresses?  Don't many devout Jews write 
the name of the Almighty as "G-d"?  How can we possibly please everyone?

>Henry DeVries, computer specialist for cooperative education, suggested
>implementation of a training program for system administrators which would
>teach them the specifics of obscenity codes and laws.

Translation: "teach them the *local* definition of obscenity".  That's an
essential distinction in this situation.

As I understand it, an essential part of the definition of obscenity is 
"community standards".  Resources such as email, Usenet, FTP, Gopher, and
WAIS (to name just a few) span hundreds of thousands of communities in the
United States alone; can we hope to find common ground among them?  I don't
think it possible.  If we include the global particpants, we are confronted 
with a dizzying array of cultural and societal taboos.  Attempting to control 
global resources with local law/policy is a *serious* mistake.

>Lynn attempted to quell the Assembly's doubts.  "We at Cornell do not want
>to limit anyone's freedom of speech, but we are a community and we must protect
>our members," he said.

Why?  What members of the Cornell community *need* protection?  Which of them
are incapable of registering complaints and taking legal action against
"objectionable", "obscene" or "offensive" material *themselves*?  I'll assume
that Cornell has an office (or offices, perhaps) responsible for handling
complaints of discrimination and harassment; why can't system administrators
refer complaints to the appropriate office?   Cornell is asking its admins
to make judgements about the effect(s) of online material on third parties; 
that's destined to fail.

>"We do recognize that we can't always decide what is appropriate or inapp-
>ropriate, but where time is of the essense and more harm is done by allowing
>the file to remain, we must act," Lynn added.

This policy encourages capricious action.  Even when I don't agree with
actions taken by users, I rarely advocate a "quick-draw" termination of 
services or access.  Yeesh.  I also wonder about the stage at which 
"time is of the essence" when we're talking about GIFs, screen savers, 
and games. (if it was a list of stolen credit card numbers, I'd agree,
but we're talking about a completely subjective decision here.)  If a 
user was flooding a system with "hate email" to every user or sending 
real-time messages to every user with the Unix write(1) command, I could 
understand the need for "immediate action"; when we're dealing with files 
sitting on a disk, I have a hard time envisioning such a need.

By the way, what will Cornell do if a professor teaching a history course
entitled "Anti-Semitism through the Ages" puts an archive of material online
for his students?   How will this policy affect legitimate research and dis-
cussion of controversial subjects?

--Wes

